  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b5bd5e60e42c9624b876a36ed587e020, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  infoTodisplay: The American alligator (Alligator mississippiensis) resides within
    the southern extreme of the Florida Everglades. They primarily inhabit freshwater
    areas such as swamps and marshes or like, rivers and similar smalles bodies of
    freshwater. Adult male alligators occasionally reach 13 to 15 feet in length.
    Maximum length for females is approximately 10 feet. Their diet consists mostly
    of fish, turtles, small mammals, brids and reptiles. Alligators in general are
    an important part of the ecosystem as their nests serve as important for the
    creation of peat, with the adandoned nests providing a place for many animals
    to lay their eggs. Their roles as important consumers in the ecosystems food
    web also solidifies their importance to the enviornment.
  TextBoxToReveal: {fileID: 4360595044501793895}
  CanvasBase: {fileID: 5731597658798959907}
  interactAudio: {fileID: 2098033487251920132}
